-- title:
--   Coinvariants of a free monoid-algebra module: freeness, rank, descent
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal{O}$ be a nontrivial commutative ring, $\Delta$ a commutative monoid, and write $\Lambda = \mathcal{O}[\Delta]$ for the monoid algebra and $\mathfrak{a} \subseteq \Lambda$ for the kernel of the counit algebra map of the bialgebra $\Lambda$ (the augmentation, sending each element of $\Delta$ to $1$). Let $R$ be a ring that is an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra, $\iota : \Lambda \to R$ an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra homomorphism, and $M$ an abelian group carrying compatible $R$-, $\Lambda$- and $\mathcal{O}$-module structures, with $\mathcal{O}$ acting through $\Lambda$, with $M$ free as a $\Lambda$-module, and with the $\Lambda$-action given by $c \cdot m = \iota(c) \cdot m$. Let $T_0$ be a ring and $\mathcal{O}$-algebra, $\psi : R \to T_0$ an $\mathcal{O}$-algebra homomorphism, $M_0$ a $T_0$-module and $\mathcal{O}$-module, and $\pi : M \to M_0$ a surjective $\mathcal{O}$-linear map satisfying $\pi(x \cdot m) = \psi(x) \cdot \pi(m)$ for all $x \in R$, $m \in M$, whose vanishing locus is exactly the submodule $\mathfrak{a} \cdot M$ (the $\Lambda$-submodule generated by products of elements of $\mathfrak{a}$ with elements of $M$). Then the coinvariants $M / \mathfrak{a}M$ are free over $\mathcal{O}$, their $\mathcal{O}$-rank equals the $\Lambda$-rank of $M$ as cardinals, and there exists an $\mathcal{O}$-linear isomorphism $e : M/\mathfrak{a}M \to M_0$ with $e([m]) = \pi(m)$ for all $m \in M$ and $e([x \cdot m]) = \psi(x) \cdot e([m])$ for all $x \in R$, $m \in M$.
--
--   This is the module-theoretic input for passing from an augmented Taylor–Wiles level back to the minimal level: a module free over the group algebra of the diamond group has free coinvariants of the same rank, and any equivariant surjection with kernel the augmentation submodule identifies the coinvariants with its target. It is used in the construction of Taylor–Wiles modules in the patching argument.
